     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. The problem is the dose, not whether you're authorized to ask. Ibuprofen 1,600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That's double the maximum daily dose, and it could seriously harm the patient. Confirming it doesn't change that risk.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **If a different dose was intended**, tell me the dose and frequency. For example, 400 mg four times daily is 1,600 mg/day. 800 mg three times daily is 2,400 mg/day. I can enter either right away.\n- **If a prescriber really wants this dose**, they should enter it themselves. That way their clinical judgment, the pharmacy's review, and the hospital's safety checks all apply.\n- **If the goal is stronger pain or inflammation control**, the treating clinician or a pharmacist can suggest safer options. These include adding acetaminophen or choosing a different drug class."
      }
     ],
